:::python
### Handle a model refusal

:::

:::js
### Handle a model refusal

Providers report a refusal, such as a safety-policy refusal, as a successful response rather than an error. A plain model call returns the message with a [`refusal` content block](/oss/langchain/messages#content-block-reference). `withStructuredOutput` throws @[`ModelRefusalError`] from `@langchain/core/errors` instead of a parsing error. The error's `rawMessage` holds the refused response. The message's `response_metadata.normalized_finish_reason` is `"refusal"`; see [Finish reason](/oss/langchain/messages#finish-reason).

```typescript

const modelWithStructure = model.withStructuredOutput(Movie);

try {
await modelWithStructure.invoke("Provide details about the movie Inception");
} catch (error) {
if (ModelRefusalError.isInstance(error)) {
console.log(error.message); // Model refused to produce the requested output (category='cyber'): ...
const refused = error.rawMessage;
}
}
```

With `includeRaw: true`, `withStructuredOutput` does not throw. A refusal returns `parsed: null`, so check `raw` for the refusal block:

```typescript
const modelWithStructure = model.withStructuredOutput(Movie, { includeRaw: true });
const response = await modelWithStructure.invoke("Provide details about the movie Inception");
const refusal = response.raw.contentBlocks.find((block) => block.type === "refusal");
```

