### Model fallback

Automatically fallback to alternative models when the primary model fails. Model fallback is useful for the following:

@wrap_model_call
def reply_on_refusal(
request: ModelRequest,
handler: Callable[[ModelRequest], ModelResponse],
) -> ModelResponse:
try:
return handler(request)
except ModelRefusalError:
# Every model refused: reply to the user instead of failing the run
return ModelResponse(result=[AIMessage("I cannot help with that request.")])

agent = create_agent(
model="claude-sonnet-5-5",
tools=[],
middleware=[
reply_on_refusal,
ModelFallbackMiddleware("gpt-6.1-sol"),
ModelRefusalMiddleware(),
],
)
```

When `claude-sonnet-5-5` refuses, the request falls back to `gpt-6.1-sol`. If that model also refuses, `reply_on_refusal` returns the message.





For other handling, such as recording the refusal in state or calling `interrupt()` to ask a human how to continue, skip `ModelRefusalMiddleware` and check each response for a [`refusal` content block](/oss/langchain/messages#content-block-reference) in your own middleware. See [Custom middleware](/oss/langchain/middleware/custom).





:::

```typescript

createAgent,
modelFallbackMiddleware,
modelRefusalMiddleware,
} from "langchain";

const agent = createAgent({
model: "claude-sonnet-5-5",
tools: [],
middleware: [
modelFallbackMiddleware("openai:gpt-6.1-sol"),
modelRefusalMiddleware(),
],
});
```

List @[`modelRefusalMiddleware`] after @[`modelFallbackMiddleware`] so the fallback catches the error and retries on the next model. `modelRetryMiddleware` does not retry refusals. With its default `onFailure: "continue"`, it returns the error as the final answer. List it after `modelRefusalMiddleware`, or set `onFailure: "error"`. When streaming, text the model sends before refusing can reach the client before the middleware throws.

#### Handle a refusal

Choose how your application responds when the model refuses:





Catch @[`ModelRefusalError`] where you invoke the agent. The refused reply is not saved to the thread, so the next message continues the conversation normally, on the same model or a different one:

Catch @[`ModelRefusalError`] where you invoke the agent. The refused reply is not saved to the thread, so the next message continues the conversation normally, on the same model or a different one:

```typescript

const agent = createAgent({
model: "claude-sonnet-5-5",
tools: [],
middleware: [modelRefusalMiddleware()],
checkpointer: new MemorySaver(),
});
const config = { configurable: { thread_id: "1" } };

try {
await agent.invoke({ messages: [{ role: "user", content: "..." }] }, config);
} catch (error) {
if (!ModelRefusalError.isInstance(error)) throw error;
console.log(error.message); // Tell the user the request was refused
}

// The thread is still usable
await agent.invoke(
{ messages: [{ role: "user", content: "Try something else" }] },
config
);
```

